Docker Commands Every Engineer Should Know

Docker simplifies how we build, ship, and run applications. Whether you’re into DevOps, Cloud, or Backend engineering, these commands form your daily toolkit:

🔹 docker version – Check Docker client & engine details

🔹 docker info – View system-wide Docker information

🔹 docker pull <image> – Download an image from Docker Hub

🔹 docker images – List available images

🔹 docker run <image> – Create and start a container

🔹 docker ps / docker ps -a – View running / all containers

🔹 docker stop <container> – Stop a running container

🔹 docker rm <container> – Remove a container

🔹 docker rmi <image> – Remove an image

🔹 docker logs <container> – Inspect container logs

🔹 docker exec -it <container> /bin/bash – Access a running container

🔹 docker build -t app:tag . – Build an image from Dockerfile

🔹 docker compose up -d – Run multi-container apps

🔹 docker compose down – Stop and clean up services


Docker isn’t just a tool — it’s a standard for modern application delivery.
